  "details": "Title.\n\nFor those unsure of how VRChat networking works - It uses Proton. The current implementation simply uses Proton as an implementation layer and relay.\nSimply put, when a user is in a room, and someone sends network stuff, that is sent to a relay server that just blindly distributes it to other players in the room. As you'd imagine, blindly sending things on is not a very good idea.\n\nWhat should be done instead, is a system for server authority. Yes, this means more computing power required and that servers would essentially have a game state to remember per room, but it would patch out a lot of the security flaws we have with the current system. For example, someone can't just send a malicious kick moderation command anymore; there would be logic there to intercept and not send it on.\n\nFurthermore, a server to keep track of the current game state (rather than trying to get all past events from everyone else) would solve a lot of bugs with late joiners and dynamic/interactive objects in the worlds.\n\nEdit: This could be completely wrong as that is my two cents and discovery from simply de-compiling the binaries for a possible attack vector. It seems like everything is encrypted BUT the SDK binds, Unity itself and any external library (like Proton and Steamworks.NET (website is: https://steamworks.github.io/)\n\nIf you can debunk or confirm my suspicions, that would be great.",
